<section class="section-tight" style="padding-top:0">
  <div class="wrap bi-grid">
    <article class="article">
      <div class="article-img"><img src="../assets/img/blog/seo-nedir.svg" alt=""></div>
      <p class="bi-lead">Most of your customers type something into Google before they find you: “accountant near me”, “corporate website price”, “apartment dues software”. <b>SEO</b> is the name for the work that determines whether your site shows up in front of them at the moment they search.</p>
      <p>In this article we answer the question <b>what is SEO</b> without drowning you in jargon: how Google discovers a page, what it ranks it by, how AI summaries (AI Overviews and AI Mode) changed the game in 2025–2026, and which steps you should take in the first month as a business owner.</p>
      <p>We have three more articles that cover the topic in depth, and we’ll point you to them along the way. Think of this article as the introduction that shows the whole map.</p>
      <h2 id="tanim" class="bi-h2" data-n="01">What is SEO? A short, clear definition</h2>
      <p>SEO stands for <b>Search Engine Optimization</b>, i.e. <b>optimising for search engines</b>. Its aim is to make your site more visible in relevant searches without paying for ads, that is, <b>organically</b>.</p>
      <p>Google’s own definition is similar: SEO is the process of helping search engines understand your content and helping users find your site and decide whether to click on it. Note that there are two parties in this definition: the search engine and the human. Good SEO serves both.</p>
      <p>Most of the search market in Türkiye is on Google. According to Statcounter data for September 2026, Google’s share of the search engine market in Türkiye is about 73 percent and Yandex’s about 25 percent. That’s why this guide focuses on Google; however, most of the principles described also apply to Yandex.</p>
      <div class="bi-note"><svg class="ic" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M13 2L4 14h7l-1 8 9-12h-7l1-8z"/></svg><div><b>Keep in mind:</b> SEO is not a plugin, a setting or a one-off package. It is ongoing work on your site’s structure, content and reputation.</div></div>
      <h2 id="google-nasil-calisir" class="bi-h2" data-n="02">How does Google find, store and rank a page?</h2>
      <p>Google Search Central documentation describes the process in three stages. Almost every technical SEO topic touches one of these three stages:</p>
      <ul class="bi-list">
        <li><svg class="ic" width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M5 12l5 5 9-10"/></svg><span><b>1. Crawling —</b> An automated program called Googlebot discovers and downloads new and updated pages by following links and sitemaps.</span></li>
        <li><svg class="ic" width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M5 12l5 5 9-10"/></svg><span><b>2. Indexing —</b> Google analyses the text, images and headings on the page, works out what the page is about and stores it in its huge database (the index). Not every crawled page gets indexed.</span></li>
        <li><svg class="ic" width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M5 12l5 5 9-10"/></svg><span><b>3. Serving —</b> When someone searches, Google ranks the pages in its index it considers most useful for that person. Hundreds of factors play a role here, such as the meaning of the query, location, language and device.</span></li>
      </ul>
      <p>The practical upshot: if Google can’t reach your page (crawling), can’t understand it (indexing), or your page doesn’t answer the question better than competitors (ranking), you are invisible. Knowing which stage the problem is in also determines the fix.</p>
      <p>Google also states plainly that you can’t pay Google to rank higher. Ads are a separate area and do not affect organic results.</p>
      <h2 id="uc-ayak" class="bi-h2" data-n="03">The three pillars of SEO: technical, content and authority</h2>
      <p>SEO work is usually grouped under three headings. Neglect one and the impact of the other two is limited too.</p>
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Pillar</th><th>What does it mean?</th><th>Example tasks</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td><b>Technical SEO</b></td><td>Making sure Google can crawl and index the site without problems</td><td>robots.txt, sitemap, HTTPS, speed, mobile-friendliness, redirects</td></tr>
          <tr><td><b>Content and on-page SEO</b></td><td>Giving a good answer to the right question with the right page</td><td>Keyword and intent analysis, title tags, copy, images, internal links</td></tr>
          <tr><td><b>Authority (off-page SEO)</b></td><td>How well known and trusted your site is in the outside world</td><td>Natural links, brand mentions, reviews, industry directories</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
      <p>If you want to check the technical side item by item, you can apply <a href="teknik-seo-kontrol-listesi">our technical SEO checklist</a> step by step. If you have a physical shop or serve a specific area, a large part of the authority pillar revolves around maps and reviews; we cover this in detail <a href="yerel-seo-google-isletme-profili">in our local SEO and Google Business Profile guide</a>.</p>
      <h2 id="anahtar-kelime" class="bi-h2" data-n="04">Keywords and search intent: choosing the right question</h2>
      <p>The starting point of SEO is knowing which words your customers search with. But more important than the word itself is the <b>intent</b> behind it. Two searches on the same topic can want very different things:</p>
      <ul class="bi-list">
        <li><svg class="ic" width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M5 12l5 5 9-10"/></svg><span><b>Informational intent:</b> “what is SEO”, “how are dues calculated”. The person wants to learn and expects a guide or explanatory article.</span></li>
        <li><svg class="ic" width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M5 12l5 5 9-10"/></svg><span><b>Commercial investigation:</b> “best POS software”, “WordPress or custom software”. The person is comparing options.</span></li>
        <li><svg class="ic" width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M5 12l5 5 9-10"/></svg><span><b>Transactional intent:</b> “I want a website built”, “restaurant POS system price”. The person is close to buying or asking for a quote.</span></li>
        <li><svg class="ic" width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M5 12l5 5 9-10"/></svg><span><b>Local intent:</b> “Kadıköy dental clinic”, “car service near me”. The person is looking for a nearby business.</span></li>
      </ul>
      <p>Each intent needs a different type of page. Trying to show your service page to people seeking information, or your blog post to people requesting a quote, doesn’t work. Searching a keyword on Google and looking at the first page of results is the fastest way to understand its intent.</p>
      <h3>Why are long-tail keywords valuable?</h3>
      <p>For generic keywords like “website”, competition is very high and what the searcher wants is unclear. Long-tail searches like “price of a corporate website in Istanbul” have lower volume, but the searcher knows what they want. For new and small sites, realistic gains usually come from here.</p>
      <h2 id="icerik-kalitesi" class="bi-h2" data-n="05">What content does Google consider “helpful”?</h2>
      <p>Google states clearly that its ranking systems aim to surface reliable content created to help people. The essence of the questions in its “Creating helpful, reliable, people-first content” document is: does this page really do the job for the reader, or was it written only for the search engine?</p>
      <p>An acronym you’ll often hear in this context is <b>E-E-A-T</b>: experience, expertise, authoritativeness and trust. These aren’t individually measured scores but qualities that good content has. On a business website you can show them like this:</p>
      <ul class="bi-list">
        <li><svg class="ic" width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M5 12l5 5 9-10"/></svg><span>Real project examples, your own photos and concrete figures</span></li>
        <li><svg class="ic" width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M5 12l5 5 9-10"/></svg><span>Information showing who wrote the article and what experience it is based on</span></li>
        <li><svg class="ic" width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M5 12l5 5 9-10"/></svg><span>Clear contact details, address, company information and references</span></li>
        <li><svg class="ic" width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M5 12l5 5 9-10"/></svg><span>Adding your own knowledge and perspective instead of repeating what competitors say</span></li>
      </ul>
      <div class="bi-note"><svg class="ic" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M12 3l8 3v6c0 4.5-3.4 8.3-8 9-4.6-.7-8-4.5-8-9V6l8-3z"/><path d="M9 12l2 2 4-4"/></svg><div><b>Warning:</b> Google’s spam policies explicitly prohibit producing large numbers of low-value pages (with AI or by hand) to manipulate rankings. AI can be used as a tool; the problem is not the tool itself but content that benefits no one.</div></div>
      <h2 id="yapay-zeka" class="bi-h2" data-n="06">How are AI searches affecting SEO?</h2>
      <p>2025 and 2026 were years of major change in search. Google’s AI summaries (AI Overviews, also known as <b>AI Bakışı</b> in Turkish) and the conversational <b>AI Mode</b> were gradually rolled out in Türkiye from February 2026. Answers to many questions are now summarised above the results list, together with source links.</p>
      <p>This is changing click behaviour. In a 2025 Pew Research Center study using the browsing data of 900 adults in the US, users clicked on a traditional result in 8 percent of visits to searches showing an AI summary, compared with 15 percent for searches without one. So expect fewer clicks, especially for simple informational questions.</p>
      <p>So what should you do? Google’s “AI features and your website” document is clear: there are <b>no additional requirements or special optimisations</b> to appear in AI Overviews or AI Mode. It is enough for the page to be indexed and eligible to be shown with a snippet; no special AI file or special schema markup is required. The fundamentals of SEO apply here too:</p>
      <ul class="bi-list">
        <li><svg class="ic" width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M5 12l5 5 9-10"/></svg><span>Write important information as text, not inside images</span></li>
        <li><svg class="ic" width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M5 12l5 5 9-10"/></svg><span>Ask questions clearly and answer them directly in the first paragraph, then give the detail</span></li>
        <li><svg class="ic" width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M5 12l5 5 9-10"/></svg><span>Add information based on your own experience that can’t be found elsewhere; this is the content summaries want to link to as a source</span></li>
        <li><svg class="ic" width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M5 12l5 5 9-10"/></svg><span>AI traffic doesn’t appear as a separate report in Search Console but is included in the performance data for the “Web” search type; interpret your measurements accordingly</span></li>
      </ul>
      <blockquote><div class="bi-q"><svg class="ic" width="22" height="22" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M9 7H5a1 1 0 0 0-1 1v4a1 1 0 0 0 1 1h3v1a3 3 0 0 1-3 3M19 7h-4a1 1 0 0 0-1 1v4a1 1 0 0 0 1 1h3v1a3 3 0 0 1-3 3"/></svg></div>SEO hasn’t died in the age of AI; the value of ordinary content has fallen and the value of genuinely helpful content has risen.</blockquote>
      <h2 id="sure-olcum" class="bi-h2" data-n="07">How long does SEO take to deliver results, and how is it measured?</h2>
      <p>The honest answer: it varies. With a new site, a competitive industry and authority built from scratch, meaningful organic traffic usually takes months. Removing a technical barrier (for example, pages accidentally left on “noindex”), on the other hand, can make a difference within a few weeks. Google itself recommends asking an SEO consultant “what results do you expect, and in what timeframe?” and adds: no one can guarantee a number one ranking on Google.</p>
      <p>Two free tools are enough for measurement:</p>
      <ul class="bi-list">
        <li><svg class="ic" width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M5 12l5 5 9-10"/></svg><span><b>Google Search Console:</b> Shows which searches you appear in, how many impressions and clicks you get, and indexing problems.</span></li>
        <li><svg class="ic" width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M5 12l5 5 9-10"/></svg><span><b>Google Analytics 4:</b> Shows what visitors do on the site and whether they fill in a form or call.</span></li>
      </ul>
      <p>Measure success by business, not rankings: quote requests, phone calls and sales from organic search. For how to balance advertising and SEO when you need quick results, see <a href="google-ads-mi-seo-mu">Google Ads or SEO</a>.</p>
      <h2 id="ilk-adimlar" class="bi-h2" data-n="08">What to do in the first 30 days for your business</h2>
      <p>A practical sequence for businesses that don’t know where to start with SEO:</p>
      <ul class="bi-list">
        <li><svg class="ic" width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M5 12l5 5 9-10"/></svg><span><b>Week 1 —</b> Set up Google Search Console and Analytics, submit your sitemap and note the errors in the “Page indexing” report.</span></li>
        <li><svg class="ic" width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M5 12l5 5 9-10"/></svg><span><b>Week 2 —</b> List 3–5 searches customers use for each of your services and check whether you have a matching page for each.</span></li>
        <li><svg class="ic" width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M5 12l5 5 9-10"/></svg><span><b>Week 3 —</b> Rewrite the title and description tags, H1 headings and copy of your main pages around these searches. If you serve a local area, verify your Google Business Profile.</span></li>
        <li><svg class="ic" width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M5 12l5 5 9-10"/></svg><span><b>Week 4 —</b> Publish your first guide answering a question your customers ask most often, and link to it from your service pages.</span></li>
      </ul>
      <p>If your site is about to be built, it is much cheaper to lay these foundations from the start than to add them later; our article <a href="kurumsal-web-sitesi-rehberi">what you need to know before commissioning a corporate website</a> is a good starting point in this respect.</p>
      <div class="bi-check">
        <div class="eyebrow">// CHECKLIST BEFORE STARTING SEO</div>
        <ul class="bi-list"><li><svg class="ic" width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M5 12l5 5 9-10"/></svg><span>Are Search Console and Analytics set up and verified?</span></li><li><svg class="ic" width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M5 12l5 5 9-10"/></svg><span>Is there a separate, substantial page for each service?</span></li><li><svg class="ic" width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M5 12l5 5 9-10"/></svg><span>Do page titles and descriptions reflect what customers search for?</span></li><li><svg class="ic" width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M5 12l5 5 9-10"/></svg><span>Does the site load fast and work comfortably on a phone?</span></li><li><svg class="ic" width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M5 12l5 5 9-10"/></svg><span>Is important information written as text rather than in images?</span></li><li><svg class="ic" width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M5 12l5 5 9-10"/></svg><span>Are you measuring success by leads and sales rather than rankings?</span></li></ul>
      </div>
      <h2 id="sss" class="bi-h2" data-n="09">Frequently asked questions</h2>
      <div class="bi-faq"><details class="bi-faq-i"><summary>What is SEO and what does it do?</summary><p>SEO, or search engine optimisation, is the combination of technical, content and authority work that makes your website more visible in relevant searches on search engines like Google without paying for ads. Its aim is to bring potential customers who are searching for you to your site.</p></details><details class="bi-faq-i"><summary>Can you do SEO yourself?</summary><p>The basic steps can be done yourself: setting up Search Console, editing page titles, creating a Google Business Profile and writing content that answers customer questions. For technical issues, site architecture and competitive industries, however, help from an experienced team saves time.</p></details><details class="bi-faq-i"><summary>How long does SEO take to deliver results?</summary><p>It depends on the site’s age, the competition in the industry and the technical state at the start. Removing technical barriers can have an effect within a few weeks, while meaningful organic traffic on a new site usually takes months. Be wary of services that guarantee an exact timeframe or a number one ranking.</p></details><details class="bi-faq-i"><summary>Have AI searches killed SEO?</summary><p>No. According to Google, there are no additional requirements to appear in AI Overviews and AI Mode; pages that are indexed and offer good content can be shown as sources in these summaries. Clicks may fall for simple informational questions, but the value of original, experience-based content is rising.</p></details><details class="bi-faq-i"><summary>What is the difference between SEO and Google Ads?</summary><p>With Google Ads you pay per click and appear in the ad slots immediately, and visibility stops when the budget runs out. With SEO you don’t pay for clicks, but getting results takes time and effort; the visibility you earn is more lasting. Many businesses use both together.</p></details></div>
      <h2 id="sonuc">Final word</h2>
      <p>To sum up SEO: make sure Google can reach your site, write the page that best answers the customer’s question and earn trust over time. AI summaries didn’t change these principles; they only raised the cost of settling for ordinary content.</p>
      <p>At CYF Tech we build SEO into our sites as part of the architecture, not as a service added later. If you’re wondering why your current site isn’t visible, or want to start a new site with an SEO foundation, <a href="iletisim">write to us</a>; let’s take a look together.</p>
